    id: "derivations",
    title: "What is a Derivation?",
    group: "Module 2 — Derivations & Packaging Fundamentals",
    summary: "The build recipe at the heart of everything Nix does.",
    content: [
      { t: "p", text: "A derivation is Nix's word for a build recipe: it describes the source code, the build commands, and the dependencies needed to produce an output. When you write a Nix package, you're writing a derivation (usually through a helper like stdenv.mkDerivation)." },
      { t: "h", text: "The isolated build" },
      { t: "p", text: "Nix builds derivations in a sandbox: no network access, and no access to files beyond the declared inputs. The build cannot reach out and grab anything unexpected — if it needs a dependency, you must declare it. That discipline is what makes the result reproducible." },
      { t: "note", text: "The one exception to the no-network rule is a fixed-output derivation (FOD), whose output hash is pre-declared — source fetchers like fetchurl use this so they're allowed to download a tarball while Nix still verifies its hash." },
      { t: "h", text: "The output goes into the store" },
      { t: "p", text: "Every build result lands in /nix/store/<hash>-<name>-<version>/ where the hash is computed from the derivation's inputs. Change any input — a dependency version, a flag, a source — and you get a different store path. Same inputs always give the same path." },
      { t: "read", code: "/nix/store/4xw8k9r3abc…-hello-2.12.1/bin/hello" },
      { t: "h", text: "The $out variable" },
      { t: "p", text: "Inside a build, $out is the output path in the store. The golden rule: every artifact you produce must be placed under $out, and nothing outside it (the sandbox wouldn't allow it anyway)." },
      { t: "read", code: "installPhase = ''\n  mkdir -p $out/bin\n  cp my-program $out/bin/\n'';" },
      { t: "h", text: "Inspecting builds" },
      { t: "read", code: "nix log .#my-package      # Read the build log of a derivation\nnix path-info -r ./result  # Show the runtime closure (all dependencies)\nnix-store -q --tree ./result  # Classic way to see the dependency tree" },
    ],
